<section class='slide about-slide' id="features">
  <div class='about-slide__wrapper'>
    <div class='about-slide__section'>
      <div class='about-slide__content about-slide__content-full'>
        <h2 class='about-slide__title'>
          Instant, scalable, dependable. And yours.
        </h2>
        <div class='about-slide__pillars'>
          <article class='about-slide__pillar'>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-label'>Instant</p>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-figure'>3 ms<small>p50</small></p>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-caption'>roundtrip at 10K connections, 11 ms at p99</p>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-text'>
              A Go server holds the sockets and fans out every broadcast. Your app publishes over HTTP, Redis or the embedded NATS, then goes back to its own work.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class='about-slide__pillar'>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-label'>Scalable</p>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-figure'>18 KB</p>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-caption'>per idle connection on Pro, against about 52 KB on Socket.io</p>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-text'>
              Connections live outside your app processes, so a launch-day spike lands on AnyCable, and you scale it by adding instances. Pro adapts its concurrency to load.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class='about-slide__pillar'>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-label'>Dependable</p>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-figure'>100%</p>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-caption'>delivered through Wi-Fi drops, against 85% to 87% for Socket.io and uWebSockets.js</p>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-text'>
              Stream history replays what a client missed, in order, when it reconnects. Connections stay open while you deploy, so users never notice a release.
            </p>
          </article>
          <article class='about-slide__pillar'>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-label'>Yours</p>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-figure'>$0</p>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-caption'>per connection or message, with no metering and no telemetry</p>
            <p class='about-slide__pillar-text'>
              Run it in your cluster under the MIT license, or self-host Pro, priced by the number of servers, with unlimited connections from $1,490 a year. Messages never leave your infrastructure.
            </p>
          </article>
        </div>
        <a class='about-slide__more plausible-event-name=Navigation plausible-event-link=features-benchmark' href='/compare/nodejs-websocket' data-ph-capture-attribute-link-id='features-benchmark'>
          How we measured: the 2026 benchmark and its open-source repo →
        </a>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='about-slide__section'>
      <div class='about-slide__content about-slide__content-full'>
        <h3 class='about-slide__heading'>What happens when…</h3>
        <div class='about-slide__table-wrap'>
          <table class='about-slide__table'>
            <thead>
              <tr>
                <th scope='col'>When</th>
                <th scope='col'>WebSockets inside your app (Action Cable, Socket.io)</th>
                <th scope='col'>AnyCable</th>
              </tr>
            </thead>
            <tbody>
              <tr>
                <td>You deploy your app</td>
                <td data-label='WebSockets in your app'>Every connection drops, and every client reconnects at once</td>
                <td data-label='AnyCable'>Connections stay open: the Go server runs apart from your app</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>A phone loses signal for 30 seconds</td>
                <td data-label='WebSockets in your app'>Messages sent in that window are gone</td>
                <td data-label='AnyCable'>The client reconnects, restores its session and receives what it missed from stream history</td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>A WebSocket server restarts</td>
                <td data-label='WebSockets in your app'>Its clients reconnect into an empty state</td>
                <td data-label='AnyCable'>Slow drain spreads the reconnects out, and clients resume from stream history shared across the <span class='about-slide__nowrap'>cluster <span class='about-slide__pro'>Pro</span></span></td>
              </tr>
              <tr>
                <td>Launch-day traffic arrives</td>
                <td data-label='WebSockets in your app'>App processes hold the connections and run short of memory</td>
                <td data-label='AnyCable'>Add AnyCable instances; your app's memory stays flat</td>
              </tr>
            </tbody>
          </table>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    {{!-- TODO(fastapi): link a FastAPI package and example app here and from
         the docs card in resources-slide.hbs once they ship. Until then the
         sample signs streams by hand, the way the Python guide does. --}}
    <div class='about-slide__section' id='stack'>
      <div class='about-slide__content'>
        <h3 class='about-slide__heading'>Works with your stack</h3>
        <p class='about-slide__text about-slide__text_lead'>
          AnyCable runs next to your application. Any backend that can send an HTTP request can broadcast, Node.js included, and Rails and Laravel get native SDKs. Hotwire, Turbo Streams and every JavaScript framework work out of the box, and if you already run Redis or NATS, AnyCable can use them.
        </p>
        <h3 class='about-slide__subtitle'>Delivery guarantees, out of the box</h3>
        <p class='about-slide__text'>
          Chat messages, edits in a shared document and notifications reach every user in order. When a phone loses signal on the subway or switches from Wi-Fi to cellular, AnyCable replays what it missed as soon as it reconnects, and connections stay open through your deploys.
        </p>
        <h3 class='about-slide__subtitle'>Streamed LLM answers resume after a dropped connection</h3>
        <p class='about-slide__text'>
          A tab switch or a network blip cuts off a streamed LLM answer. With AnyCable, your backend broadcasts tokens to a stream, and the Go server holds the connection, keeps the history and replays from where the client left off.
          <a href='https://docs.anycable.io/guides/python' target='_blank' class='plausible-event-name=Navigation plausible-event-link=fastapi-guide' data-ph-capture-attribute-link-id='fastapi-guide'>Read the Python and FastAPI guide →</a>
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class='about-slide__media about-slide__media-align-top about-slide__media-code' data-controller="tabs">
        <div class='about-slide__tabs'>
          <button data-tabs-target="code-pubsub" class='button button_variant_outlined about-slide__tab active-tab'>Browser</button>
          <button data-tabs-target="code-js" class='button button_variant_outlined about-slide__tab'>Node.js</button>
          <button data-tabs-target="code-rb" class='button button_variant_outlined about-slide__tab'>Rails</button>
          <button data-tabs-target="code-py" class='button button_variant_outlined about-slide__tab'>FastAPI</button>
        </div>
        <div class='slide-show__frame' id="codes">
          <div data-tabs-id="code-pubsub" class='slide-show__code-wrapper'>
{{> slides/code-snippets/pubsub}}
          </div>
          <div data-tabs-id="code-js" style="display:none" class='slide-show__code-wrapper'>
{{> slides/code-snippets/js}}
          </div>
          <div data-tabs-id="code-rb" style="display:none" class='slide-show__code-wrapper'>
{{> slides/code-snippets/ruby}}
          </div>
          <div data-tabs-id="code-py" style="display:none" class='slide-show__code-wrapper'>
{{> slides/code-snippets/python}}
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class='about-slide__section'>
      <div class='about-slide__content'>
        <h3 class='about-slide__heading'>Managed AnyCable, Pro, or open source</h3>
        <p class='about-slide__text'>
          Start on Managed AnyCable with two free cables. Move to self-hosted Pro when you want it on your own infrastructure, or run open source for free. All three speak the same protocol, so moving between them takes no code changes.
        </p>
        <h3 class='about-slide__subtitle'>Your messages stay on your servers</h3>
        <p class='about-slide__text'>
          Self-hosted Pro and open source keep every realtime message, chat history included, inside your infrastructure. Nothing goes to a third party, which is why healthcare teams run AnyCable for HIPAA-covered data.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class='about-slide__media'>
        <div class='slide-show__frame'>{{inline
          'images/illustration.svg'
        }}</div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
